Findings and methods we build on
--------------------------------
Facts, methods and ideas from these projects are used in our own code and
cited in the notes where they are used.

dn2_firmware_explore (https://github.com/angellinares/dn2_firmware_explore,
by angellinares, AGPL-3.0 or later), the Digitone II 1.11 mod project.
We use their published findings, credited; none of their code is included.
  - T3.8, our effects in the DSP section: the splice method (new load
    blocks before FINAL, code in L1 block 1 0x2dd600-0x2e7000 below the DM
    cache), the 1 MiB limit on section 7, the cache map, and their silicon
    finding that selas mis-encodes a shift by a negative immediate. The
    encoding audit (tools/dspsplice/audit.py) is their idea, our code.
  - T3.2 and T6.0: the SHARC clock (1 GHz) and the free L1 range; the
    working project image and the kit and sound storage facts.
  - T5.0, the FX page UI: the page records, the page-id lists and their
    36 bound, relabelling by repointing descriptor strings, and the dead
    ERR rows of the parameter table.
  - T6.1 and T6.5, the parameter table: they grew it first, on 1.11, for
    their lfo4 mod (moved table, ten added rows, a display reader divert,
    the map builder's bound kept at 321 while slots pass 100). Their row
    layout, the 56 base references, the bound counts, the slot tables and
    the lock id map were our starting facts.
  - T2.x: the arpeggiator patch points on MIDI tracks match their midiarp
    (we reached them independently, and use their design notes).

digikit (above): the ColdFire emulator our fork extends, the SHARC+ table
decoder, the ColdfireEMAC Ghidra language and McfLabels.java.

elekloader (above): the mod format, the core and hook model, and the
device profiles our Digitone II 1.12 profile follows.

selache (above): selcc, selas and seld build every piece of our DSP code;
its encoding tables (visa.rs), loader format (ldr.rs) and target notes
(target.rs) are cited in tools/sharcvm and tools/dspsplice; its 2,039 test
programs check our SHARC interpreter (read from the user's clone, not
included).

elektron-firmware-tool (https://github.com/mischa85/elektron-firmware-tool,
(c) 2026 Marcel Bierling, MIT): the .syx and ELE3 formats, and the rebuild
and seal; it is run as a program, none of its code is included.

octabam (https://github.com/sambanks/octabam, (c) 2026 Sam Banks, MIT):
the template for the overall approach.

capicola (https://github.com/heavylight-industries/capicola, AGPL-3.0):
DSP ideas we studied for the effects; no code.

The modwerk community (https://modwerk.app): shared knowledge about
Elektron firmware.


Elektron
--------
Elektron and Digitone are trademarks of Elektron Music Machines. This
project is not affiliated with or endorsed by Elektron.

No Elektron firmware is distributed here. Every build happens on the
user's machine, from the stock OS file the user downloads from Elektron.
Some scripts and notes quote short runs of stock bytes (at most 30) to
check that a patch lands where intended, and analysis/ lists the C++
class names and addresses recovered from the OS's own type information,
for interoperability.
